#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1b5ecc45-95ec-55c0-86bf-bbaeffef4049" :knowledge-point/title "Constructing a Sum of Line Integrals With Respect to X, Y, and Z"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "edd967b6-defd-518a-9713-edf26a3f6379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}3t^{2} + 2dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177671} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5abaf6-94fd-5705-85f2-f0ee69c7b76d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}t^{3} - 2\\ln tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177673} {:answer/id #uuid "6bf71bf1-ca06-54ad-ab7e-527d975daca6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}t^{2} + 5\\ln tdt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177674} {:answer/id #uuid "7c34fd97-c8e2-5fac-abd6-06191a98e6c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∫_{1}^{3}5\\ln t + 2dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186177675} {:answer/id #uuid "adf2428c-afe9-5781-be68-302512a63198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}6t^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adf2428c-afe9-5781-be68-302512a63198" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{3}6t^{3}\\,dt" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232883} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4b5b1f8-2205-55dd-8481-133a50f9b030" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336846}] :question/id #uuid "416f9a6e-1aee-5dd5-ad70-acf59db83c22" :question/math-academy-id "q-124871" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}2y\\,dx+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,dy+3z\\,dz,$ where $C$ is a portion of the curve $r(t)=\\,i+t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k$ for $t\\in [1,3]?$" :question/worked-solution "Along $C$, $x(t)=1$, $y(t)=t$, and $z(t)=t^2$, so $x'(t)=0$, $y'(t)=1$, and $z'(t)=2t$. Substitute both the coordinates and their derivatives:\n\n$$\\int_C 2y\\,dx+\\ln x\\,dy+3z\\,dz\n=\\int_1^3\\left(2t\\cdot0+\\ln(1)\\cdot1+3t^2\\cdot2t\\right)\\,dt\n=\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt.$$\n\nThus the equivalent integral is $\\int_1^3 6t^3\\,dt$, and its value is $[\\tfrac32t^4]_1^3=120$.\n\nThe original source accepted $\\int_1^3 6t^2\\,dt$ and its worked solution changed $3t^2\\cdot2t$ into $6t^2$. That simplification loses a factor of $t$. The study choice replaces that exponent with $3$; all other original choices are retained."}]}
